== Epistle to the Reader (1559)

\[Prefixed to the last edition, revised by the author.\]

#smallcaps[In] the first edition of this work, since I had not the least expectation of the success which God, in his boundless goodness, has been pleased to give it, I had for the most part done my task in a perfunctory way (as is usual in minor undertakings). But when I learned that it had been received by almost all the pious with a favor I had never dared to ask, much less to hope for, then the more sincerely I felt that this reception was beyond what I deserved, the greater I thought my ingratitude would be if I did not try to respond, at least according to my humble ability, to the very kind wishes expressed toward me, which seemed of their own accord to urge me to diligence. I attempted this not only in the second edition; in every edition since, the work has received some improvement. But though I do not regret the labor spent on it before, I was never satisfied until the work was arranged in the order in which it now appears. Now I trust it will commend itself to the judgment of all my readers. As clear proof of the diligence with which I have labored to do this service for the Church of God, I may be allowed to mention that last winter, when I thought I was dying of quartan fever, the more the illness pressed on me, the less I spared myself, so that I might leave this book behind me and so make some return to the pious for their kind urging. I would have liked to give it sooner, but it is soon enough if good enough. I will think it has appeared at the right time when I see that it brings more benefit to the Church of God than before. This is my only wish.

And truly it would go badly with me if, not content with the approval of God alone, I could not despise the foolish and perverse judgments of ignorant men as well as the malicious and unjust judgments of ungodly men. For although God has wholly devoted my mind to the desire of advancing his kingdom and promoting the public good—although my conscience is perfectly clear, and I take God and his angels as witnesses, that ever since I took up the office of teacher in the Church my only aim has been to do good to the Church by upholding the pure doctrine of piety—yet I believe there never was a man more assailed, stung, and torn by slander#footnote[Here the French original adds: “Tant des ennemis manifestes de la vérité de Dieu, que de beaucoup de canailles qui se sont fourrez en son Eglise: tant des Moines qui ont apporté leurs frocs hors de leurs cloistres pour infecter le lieu où ils venoyent, que d’autres vilains qui ne valent pas mieux qu’eux.” That is, “both by the open enemies of the truth of God and by many worthless people who have crept into his Church—both by monks who have brought their frocks out of their cloisters to spread infection wherever they come, and by other villains no better than they.” (Beveridge’s note.)]. After this letter to the reader was already in the press, I learned for certain that at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was being held, a rumor of my going over to the papacy had been spread, and was welcomed in the courts of the princes more eagerly than was right.#footnote[The words in the French are, “Avec trop grande facilité; ce qui monstroit que beaucoup de meschans hypocrites, faisans profession de l’Evangile, eussent bien voulu qu’ainsi fust.” With too great readiness; which showed that many wicked hypocrites, who professed the gospel, would have been very glad if it had been so. (Beveridge’s note.)] This, of course, is the return I get from those who are certainly well aware of many proofs of my constancy—proofs which, as they refute so foul a charge, ought to have defended me against it before all humane and impartial judges. But the devil, with all his crew, is mistaken if he imagines that by assailing me with vile falsehoods he can either cool my zeal or slacken my efforts. I trust that God, in his infinite goodness, will enable me to persevere with steady patience in the course of his holy calling. Of this I give the pious reader a new proof in the present edition.

I may add that my aim in this work has been to prepare and train students of sacred theology for the study of the sacred volume, so that they may both find an easy way into it and go on in it without stumbling. For, if I am not mistaken, I have given a summary of religion in all its parts, and arranged it in such an order that anyone who rightly grasps it will find it easy to determine both what he ought chiefly to look for in Scripture and to what end he ought to refer whatever it contains. Having thus, as it were, paved the way, I will keep any Commentaries on Scripture that I may publish later within a narrow compass, since it will be unnecessary in them to enter into long discussions of doctrines and to enlarge on doctrinal topics. In this way the pious reader will be spared much trouble and weariness, provided he comes prepared with a knowledge of the present work as an indispensable first step. Since the method followed here is set out, as in a mirror, in all my Commentaries, I think it better to let it speak for itself than to explain it in words.

Farewell, kind reader. If you gain any benefit from my labors, help me with your prayers to our heavenly Father.

#smallcaps[Geneva], 1#emph[st August] 1559.

#quote[The zeal of those whose cause I undertook,
Has swelled a short defense into a book.]

“I profess to be one of those who write as they advance, and advance by writing.”—#emph[Augustine], Epist. 7.
